@stephentoub This documentation will need to be updated:

https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/nullable-migration-strategies#generic-definitions-and-nullability

> What it does mean is that you can't use T? in a generic class or method declaration without constraints. For example, Enumerable.FirstOrDefault<TSource>(IEnumerable<TSource>) won't be changed to return T?.

review_comments:
Note this change.  It turns out Roslyn hasn't been validating the [AllowNull] here (https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/46459).  When I switched from `[AllowNull]T` to `T?`, we started getting warnings elsewhere because of code like:
```C#
List<string> list = ...;
list.Sort((x, y) => x.CompareTo(y));
```
so while technically to match `IComparer<T>` this should use `T?`, I believe it's better to use `T`.  If others disagree, obviously it can be changed.
